00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
let's speak to the chancellor rishi  sunay who joins us once again  on the programme chancellor uh really  good to see you this morning and  no england shirt on i'm gobsmacked but  but i have to say very pleased to see  you  were you up late last night  i i i was i am at least wearing a red  tie  neil so that's my my attempt at wearing  the england shirt during my uh my work  day  but yeah i know i was up late absolutely  overjoyed tense at times  obviously but fantastic night great for  the country  first time in my lifetime we'll be in a  final of a major competition so can't  wait  and and as regards that final i mean is  it coming home do you think you can do  it  i you know i do i feel good i mean i i  was euro 96 was my  football tournament coming of age i  remember it passionately  it was an amazing summer it feels like  that all over again wembley last night  what i mean what a spectacle to have  everyone cheering the way they were we  haven't seen that in so long  it felt absolutely amazing it was tense  at times no point pretending otherwise  and uh maybe a bit of a soft penalty but  we got there in the end  and i just watching sterling run past  everybody time after time it was amazing  i am overjoyed  and you know i can't wait so just going  to point out to viewers that it was you  that said it was a soft penalty and and  not me but but  just just a reflection though chancellor  on what this might mean to the country  we've all had 16 months of a pretty  torrid time  even getting to a final is a remarkable  achievement by gareth southgate and the  boys  i i i think it's an amazing moment for  the country  and we've obviously had a it's been a  tough year for lots of uh different  reasons  but to have this to look forward to at  the end of it is  absolutely fantastic and it reminds us  of what life was like before we had to  deal with coronavirus  actually you know to have people at  football match to have our sharing and  an experience as a country together  something  that brings us together you could see  that on the fans last night you can see  that across the country households  everywhere  and i know everyone's gonna be looking  forward to sunday with an extra 45  minutes in the pub as well  indeed so i might well i can't be taking  advantage i've got to be here at seven  o'clock in the morning come monday  um but let's see of course 55 years at  55 years since your last appearance in a  major final  at just a year though uh since the  anniversary just a year though since you  launched your plan for jobs see what i  did there uh something you're  celebrating today as well i imagine  yeah i did yeah that that's right a year  ago today we launched  the plan for jobs and you know i'm  delighted that we stuck to that plan  and the plan is working i was out and  about all day yesterday  in the west midlands in wolverhampton  and every business i went to i could see  our plan in action  new kickstarters young people starting  their new jobs  doing so well apprentices learning new  skills  people who've been brought back from  furlough successfully and businesses  that now having got through the crisis  with our support  are looking to expand and grow the  unemployment numbers are  far lower than we feared a year ago when  i stood up and outlined the plan for  jobs so i am  absolutely delighted that it is working  and it doesn't end here  we've got support that continues  throughout the year and we want to drive  our recovery forward now and that's what  i know we can achieve  what we did have yesterday in the past  24 hours though was confirmation that  the 20 pounds uplifted to universal  credit is  is coming to an end it just explained  that decision because there are  a number of people including you know  colleagues within your own party in  duncan smith suggesting that it was a  way by which people could  could live with some dignity why remove  it and why remove it now  it was always meant to be a temporary  measure along with lots of other things  that we've done we've been dealing with  a  unprecedented crisis that required us to  do some extraordinary things but of  course  those things can't carry on forever  whether it's the furlough scheme  or other things so universal credit is  part of that overall package of support  but it's just one thing i think the  focus we have is trying to get as many  people as possible  into work and into well-paid work so  what you've seen the national living  wage has gone up this year that's worth  350 pounds for someone working  full time on the national living wage  and we're providing  huge number of opportunities for people  to get new skills  to upskill themselves to earn more and  to get better jobs  the prime minister's lifetime skills  guarantee ensures that everyone will  have  the chance to find a better future for  themselves and that's what we need to  focus on now that the economy is  reopening  businesses are booming and we want to  get people into work sure it is as you  say but  but a part of a package of assistance  for people but it is  a fairly fundamental and significant  part of it i mean during the liaison  committee  uh yesterday when the prime minister had  it pointed out to them if you go ahead  with this cut  unemployment support will be in real  terms at its lowest point for 30 years  that's not something any chancellor  should be proud of  well what i can say is support for  people finding  new jobs or better jobs or getting new  skills  is probably higher than it has ever been  so there are currently 10 million adults  in the uk who don't have a level three  qualification  for the first time the prime minister  outlined last year the government will  fully fund  those adults to get that qualification  that's worth three and a half thousand  pounds  roughly and if you get a qualification  like that your chance of getting a job  or earning more in a new job goes up  significantly  so that's just one example of the things  we're doing to help people we're giving  businesses  three thousand pounds to take on new  apprentices and paying 95  of the training costs for that  apprentices to create opportunities  for people there and i think that's the  right approach the best way  long term to help people is to help them  get into well-paid work  and we are throwing the kitchen sink at  that as part of our plan for jobs  and we did it early i said a year ago  today we outlined the plan it's  worked over the last year and it  continues to work and we should have  confidence  that it will help drive our recovery and  help provide people with the  opportunities they need  people understand that perhaps yes in  the long term that may well be the  priority and that may will be that  that may will lead to the best outcome  but in the short term people still have  to put food on the table  shoes on their children's feet and pay  their bills and as things stand  2.2 million people receiving universal  credit right now are in work they're not  off work 2.2 million  are currently in work is there not  something structurally significantly  wrong  with our labor market when you can have  in excess of 2 million people receiving  this form of benefit  while still turning up and having a job  well we've also done some other things  to improve the generosity of our welfare  system in other ways  and actually we put an extra billion  pounds a year into something called  local housing allowance  which helps those people who are  struggling with their rent bills so  you know over over a million people will  benefit from around 600  pounds a year from that and for those  that are in work as you say the best way  to help them is to make sure their wages  rise  that's why our increase of the national  living wage is important that's worth  350 quid for someone full time on the  national living wage  and that's why giving them the  opportunity to get new skills and then  earn more in the same job or a different  job is really important  and that's why the lifetime skills  guarantee as i said that's worth three  and a half thousand pounds when  someone gets one of those qualifications  or does an apprenticeship as part of  their work  to gain some new skills that is the  right long-term way  to help people and that's where we're  investing our money  some something we've discussed in some  depth with your colleague the business  secretary yesterday um  the the delay to the changing rules  around self-isolation  there has been something of a furious  backlash from the business community  well in fact even from your own party  ian duncan smith says he won't be going  to the pub  because there's no point in doing so  because you're going to get pinged  julian metcalf the founder of pret and  itsu  said that it is completely and utterly  mental richard walker  the boss of iceland said what an s  show  but i p i appreciate people's  frustration with this  it's part of a you know what i call a  prime minister called a balanced  approach  you know we want to make sure that we  proceed with the appropriate degree of  caution and i think  what people hopefully can look at is the  enormously positive news  of the restrictions being removed so  whether you're a hospitality business  you can see now that the one meter plus  rule is going it was one of the biggest  things that businesses talk to me about  the the restrictions on the number of  people coming together  to eat at a restaurant or go out has  also a bit will  also be removed when we move to this  step these are big changes that means we  have a huge degree of freedom back and i  appreciate people's frustration  but i'd also urge them to look at the  positives as well  but just in terms of test and trace in  terms of finding people who've come into  close contact with people who test  positive for covid19 and would be  required to self-isolate  we've gotten rid of the requirement or  we will get rid of on on july the 19th  the requirement for people to check into  venues the qr codes  we know i mean it's been reported  anecdotally and it's in the papers today  that people are deleting the nhs app  on mass how will we find people that  need to self-isolate  without the ability to track people  moving in and out of hospitality venues  if they're not using the nhs app  well i trust people to exercise  appropriate responsibility and judgment  and and i know that they they will  continue to do so as they have done over  the last year and a half  but i've spoken to health secretary  about this and he's aware of the  frustrations that people have around  this  and and what you know what i'd say is  that we have two different systems  for the test and trays we have obviously  the nhs one  and then we also have the app and i know  most people's concerns rest  with how the app is working and the  health sector is aware of that  that accounts for the majority of people  who need to isolate i understand on the  numbers and he's looking at  what the most appropriate balanced and  proportionate approach to isolation is  in these circumstances uh mr surak  well done again on last night best of  luck  for sunday and thanks very much for  talking to us this morning appreciate it  thanks very much take care

